        },
        "parms": "1. Turn on the left toggle: a saw at 110 Hz on the master bus. Turn it off and turn on the right one: a sine at the same pitch, one harmonic and nothing else. Switch between them to compare.",
        "type": ".text"
      },
      "object 17": {
        "ID": 17,
        "gui": {
          "x": "560.0",
          "y": "280.0",
          "w": "300"
        },
        "parms": "2. Drag the slider: scale turns it into a cutoff from 100 to 8000 Hz for both ~lp filters, and the f box shows it. Low, the saw turns dark and round, closer to the sine. High, its buzz comes back.",
        "type": ".text"
      },
      "object 18": {
        "ID": 18,
        "gui": {
          "x": "560.0",
          "y": "410.0",
          "w": "300"
        },
        "parms": "3. The sine barely changes as you sweep: it has nothing above its pitch for the filter to take away. A filter shapes a sound by removing harmonics, so it needs a sound that has them, like the saw or ~noise.",
        "type": ".text"
      },
